142
00:11:28,533 --> 00:11:29,853
[SPEAKER_00]: But you still know what not to do.

143
00:11:31,394 --> 00:11:32,294
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't break the precepts.

144
00:11:32,334 --> 00:11:33,335
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't lose the sense of strength.

145
00:11:33,515 --> 00:11:34,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you do.

146
00:11:36,648 --> 00:11:45,436
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, when they asked the Buddha, how did you... says... the Buddha says, if you swim too hard, crossing the... crossing the river, you tire yourself against the waves and you sink.

147
00:11:46,277 --> 00:11:50,901
[SPEAKER_00]: If you swim too feebly, the waves... the... the current just sweeps you away.

148
00:11:51,001 --> 00:11:52,362
[SPEAKER_00]: If you try and stand still, you sink.

149
00:11:53,343 --> 00:11:55,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what did Master Gautama do, then, to cross the river?

150
00:11:55,866 --> 00:12:00,530
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, well, I didn't swim too hard, nor did I swim backwards, nor did I stay still.

151
00:12:01,657 --> 00:12:03,778
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say, oh, I did this fourth thing in between.

152
00:12:04,378 --> 00:12:09,140
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I did not doing of these certain things and that's... that was his practice.

153
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:23,505
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a lot subtler and a lot harder to accept, because see, now you have to live with that doubt, which is like one of the... it's quite an unpleasant hindrance, probably more than others as well, because it... it comes from your core.

154
00:12:25,650 --> 00:12:34,374
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, again, when it comes to these meditation techniques, or methods, or just the whole attitude to have something more clearly, just tell me what to do that I can stick to.

155
00:12:35,115 --> 00:12:35,555
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why.

156
00:12:36,035 --> 00:12:37,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Because this is way too uncomfortable.

157
00:12:38,556 --> 00:12:50,762
[SPEAKER_00]: But as I was describing this morning, ask yourself, hold on, but why is this, which is factually not the most painful thing, there are a lot more painful things you can imagine going through, but why is this the worst one?

158
00:12:50,802 --> 00:12:52,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is this that nobody wants to do?

159
00:12:54,529 --> 00:13:02,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it would be easier if you just hit me with a rod hundred times, than having to spend a week in a solitary confinement with a mind infected with doubt.

160
00:13:03,755 --> 00:13:04,815
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like, I'll go mad.

161
00:13:05,956 --> 00:13:10,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the hardest is just to live with yourself, while everything else is actually harder.

162
00:13:10,439 --> 00:13:11,199
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not as bad.

163
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:20,425
[SPEAKER_00]: And that should certainly be a good reason and incentive to reflect on this, because it doesn't add up, doesn't it?

164
00:13:21,431 --> 00:13:27,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Seemingly, you are your own man, in control of your choices and everything else, yet being with yourself is the worst thing.

165
00:13:28,054 --> 00:13:30,275
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind resists it the most.

166
00:13:32,176 --> 00:13:40,760
[SPEAKER_00]: And I mean, people did loads of far worse stuff, and self-torment, and even the suttas describe it, just to not have to endure it on this mental level.

167
00:13:42,101 --> 00:13:46,623
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of having this mind of doubt, nagging me for five days, I can't sleep at night,

168
00:13:48,437 --> 00:13:57,007
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll go and I'll walk 300 kilometers and live only on water and show my great resolve and determination and literally bring yourself to the brink of death.

169
00:13:57,448 --> 00:14:00,892
[SPEAKER_00]: That's easier than just staying with this mind and not trying to get rid of it.

170
00:14:02,974 --> 00:14:04,997
[SPEAKER_00]: But not breaking the precepts on account of it either.

171
00:14:10,169 --> 00:14:12,610
[SPEAKER_05]: Isn't the worst punishment in prison?

172
00:14:13,030 --> 00:14:13,291
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

173
00:14:13,371 --> 00:14:14,151
[SPEAKER_05]: Solitary confinement.

174
00:14:14,171 --> 00:14:14,411
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

175
00:14:14,431 --> 00:14:15,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was giving an example in the morning.

176
00:14:15,792 --> 00:14:19,674
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have people, they're just... All they know in their life is pain.

177
00:14:19,734 --> 00:14:20,974
[SPEAKER_00]: And they enjoy it, basically.

178
00:14:21,014 --> 00:14:27,417
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing can scare them, nothing can... You put them in a solitary confinement, then within a week, they cry like little children.

179
00:14:27,617 --> 00:14:28,338
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't handle it.

180
00:14:28,598 --> 00:14:30,118
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just too much, mentally.

181
00:14:30,339 --> 00:14:30,879
[SPEAKER_03]: Their own thoughts.

182
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:39,583
[SPEAKER_03]: That's because that the animal, the mind, it's been allowed to run wild for so long.

183
00:14:40,487 --> 00:14:44,310
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, when you're left with it, well, you become aware that you were the victim of it.

184
00:14:44,611 --> 00:14:48,294
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, you have no distractions, no one to get angry at, right?

185
00:14:48,314 --> 00:14:50,856
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, all of those outlets would exactly be that.

186
00:14:50,876 --> 00:14:54,499
[SPEAKER_03]: Just taking it away from you, where you, deep down, you know where the problem is.

187
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:57,362
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I said, everybody deep down wants peace.

188
00:14:57,482 --> 00:15:00,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people, they say, no, no, I enjoy busy life and sensuality and everything.

189
00:15:00,744 --> 00:15:07,010
[SPEAKER_00]: It's cause for them, through their view, that's where the peace will be implied, at least, or come from at the end of it.

190
00:15:08,652 --> 00:15:12,415
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the fundamental offer of sensuality, satisfaction, contentment, peace.

191
00:15:15,737 --> 00:15:16,357
[SPEAKER_03]: Every single time.

192
00:15:17,158 --> 00:15:21,421
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you realize, hold on, if I need to keep renewing this, that's not peaceful.

193
00:15:23,162 --> 00:15:24,063
[SPEAKER_03]: That's more of a trap.

194
00:15:29,947 --> 00:15:34,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, to give you another practical example in terms of the precepts, say, you take a precept to not eat afternoon.

195
00:15:37,322 --> 00:15:38,462
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, I want it past 12.

196
00:15:38,903 --> 00:15:39,783
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't actually say 12.

197
00:15:40,003 --> 00:15:40,523
[SPEAKER_03]: This is noon.

198
00:15:40,723 --> 00:15:41,283
[SPEAKER_03]: When was the noon?

199
00:15:41,303 --> 00:15:42,624
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, when the sun is the highest.

200
00:15:43,544 --> 00:15:46,165
[SPEAKER_03]: Which could be, you know, half an hour this way, half an hour that way.

201
00:15:46,225 --> 00:15:48,326
[SPEAKER_03]: But, today we have clocks, so let's say 12.

202
00:15:48,526 --> 00:15:49,126
[SPEAKER_03]: We can do 12.

203
00:15:50,067 --> 00:15:51,207
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you couldn't eat in time.

204
00:15:51,947 --> 00:15:52,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it was 12.15.

205
00:15:54,528 --> 00:15:55,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, do you eat or not?

206
00:16:01,611 --> 00:16:02,331
[SPEAKER_03]: What would you do?

207
00:16:02,871 --> 00:16:03,271
[SPEAKER_02]: I would eat.

208
00:16:03,972 --> 00:16:04,352
[SPEAKER_03]: You would eat.

209
00:16:04,472 --> 00:16:04,732
[SPEAKER_03]: And then?

210
00:16:05,928 --> 00:16:08,531
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I would try next day to be on time.

211
00:16:09,031 --> 00:16:09,572
[SPEAKER_03]: To be on time.

212
00:16:09,612 --> 00:16:10,853
[SPEAKER_03]: But what if you fail next day?

213
00:16:11,774 --> 00:16:13,755
[SPEAKER_03]: Does that mean you're not keeping that precept anymore?

214
00:16:14,656 --> 00:16:15,998
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you ate 15 minutes late?

215
00:16:17,179 --> 00:16:21,343
[SPEAKER_02]: I think if it becomes a habit, then yes.

216
00:16:22,544 --> 00:16:23,124
[SPEAKER_03]: But 12.15...

217
00:16:24,772 --> 00:16:26,092
[SPEAKER_03]: It becomes a habit of 12-15.

218
00:16:26,132 --> 00:16:26,852
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that a problem?

219
00:16:26,872 --> 00:16:28,273
[SPEAKER_03]: Why the 15 minutes on the clock?

220
00:16:28,293 --> 00:16:33,253
[SPEAKER_02]: I would say, then, I'm a person who eats 15 minutes after noon.

221
00:16:33,613 --> 00:16:34,114
[SPEAKER_03]: After the noon.

222
00:16:34,154 --> 00:16:36,654
[SPEAKER_03]: But the noon was never directly astronomically specified.

223
00:16:37,274 --> 00:16:39,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, but more or less, roughly.

224
00:16:40,155 --> 00:16:41,755
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but why is that a problem?

225
00:16:41,975 --> 00:16:42,615
[SPEAKER_03]: That's my question.

226
00:16:43,595 --> 00:16:45,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I'm not sure if it's a problem.

227
00:16:45,776 --> 00:16:49,356
[SPEAKER_02]: It depends on what was... why I wanted to do this in bed.

228
00:16:49,396 --> 00:16:49,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

229
00:16:50,656 --> 00:16:54,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you wouldn't... you shouldn't... you won't, if you start doing it rightly.

230
00:16:55,322 --> 00:16:59,125
[SPEAKER_00]: These technicalities, so to speak, will stay just that.

231
00:17:00,106 --> 00:17:07,312
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you eat... And see, one day you can eat at quarter to twelve and be within your precept and do it for completely unwholesome reasons.

232
00:17:09,074 --> 00:17:16,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the criteria for precepts, if they're done rightly, is on the internal side every single time.

233
00:17:18,295 --> 00:17:21,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not on 12 o'clock and ding-dong, you can have it now, or you can't.

234
00:17:21,896 --> 00:17:23,556
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, why am I doing this?

235
00:17:24,116 --> 00:17:25,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I eating for the right reasons?

236
00:17:25,897 --> 00:17:26,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I keep the precepts.

237
00:17:26,897 --> 00:17:28,317
[SPEAKER_00]: I always eat before 12.

238
00:17:28,678 --> 00:17:31,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, I don't have to think why I eat.

239
00:17:32,739 --> 00:17:37,060
[SPEAKER_00]: That goes back onto that mindless keeping the precept of, oh, well, my duty is fulfilled.

240
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:38,000
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

241
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Your duty starts with the precepts.

242
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:42,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Your practice begins, if, obviously, you look in the right direction.

243
00:17:43,841 --> 00:17:45,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you then say, one day you eat at 12.30,

244
00:17:47,047 --> 00:17:57,730
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you start feeling guilty, and remorseful, and feel like you broke all the precepts, because you broke the letter of the rule for 15 minutes.

245
00:18:00,070 --> 00:18:00,690
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do then?

246
00:18:00,830 --> 00:18:01,531
[SPEAKER_00]: What would you do then?

247
00:18:01,591 --> 00:18:11,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, I'll give you an example that happens to you, and you have this mind of doubt now, and feel unpleasant and guilty, because you ate, and you were not clear why you ate,

248
00:18:11,962 --> 00:18:13,262
[SPEAKER_02]: You're careless.

249
00:18:13,642 --> 00:18:18,603
[SPEAKER_02]: My approach would be to investigate why was I trying to do this in the first place.

250
00:18:18,804 --> 00:18:19,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one?

251
00:18:19,624 --> 00:18:19,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Eating?

252
00:18:20,204 --> 00:18:21,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, eating.

253
00:18:21,064 --> 00:18:21,984
[SPEAKER_00]: But it already happened.

254
00:18:22,044 --> 00:18:24,045
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't really matter now, isn't it?

255
00:18:24,165 --> 00:18:24,925
[SPEAKER_00]: It happens.

256
00:18:24,985 --> 00:18:25,925
[SPEAKER_00]: You ate.

257
00:18:25,945 --> 00:18:26,625
[SPEAKER_02]: You're remorseful.

258
00:18:26,805 --> 00:18:29,786
[SPEAKER_02]: If I know what is the goal, would it...

259
00:18:29,866 --> 00:18:32,487
[SPEAKER_02]: I may be wrong with being remorseful.

260
00:18:32,547 --> 00:18:35,007
[SPEAKER_03]: Like... Well, see, that's the point.

261
00:18:35,867 --> 00:18:36,688
[SPEAKER_03]: The remorse is there.

262
00:18:37,188 --> 00:18:38,688
[SPEAKER_03]: Whether it's right or wrong doesn't matter.

263
00:18:40,065 --> 00:18:41,026
[SPEAKER_02]: But it will go away.

264
00:18:41,367 --> 00:18:46,993
[SPEAKER_02]: And if I understand what is going on, maybe it just won't appear in the future.

265
00:18:47,814 --> 00:18:48,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

266
00:18:48,254 --> 00:18:51,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to prevent remorse from arising.

267
00:18:51,958 --> 00:18:55,482
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to prevent guilt from arising, because you don't want to have it there.

268
00:18:56,824 --> 00:18:58,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem.

269
00:18:58,305 --> 00:18:59,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't the guilt to begin with.

270
00:19:01,678 --> 00:19:03,859
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I talk about psychologizations of people.

271
00:19:04,179 --> 00:19:14,484
[SPEAKER_00]: I investigate why all of that, where I'll focus and I'll do this and I'll do that, just so that this never comes up again, because it's unpleasant.

272
00:19:14,544 --> 00:19:16,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's actually what you need to investigate.

273
00:19:17,025 --> 00:19:17,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

274
00:19:17,925 --> 00:19:22,047
[SPEAKER_00]: By default, I lean with my entire being against

275
00:19:23,377 --> 00:19:23,777
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

276
00:19:25,098 --> 00:19:25,798
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

277
00:19:25,878 --> 00:19:27,579
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you can't.

278
00:19:27,599 --> 00:19:32,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you start with the precepts.

279
00:19:33,101 --> 00:19:35,402
[SPEAKER_00]: You start with knowing what you shouldn't be doing.

280
00:19:35,942 --> 00:19:37,243
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, that's why I gave you the example.

281
00:19:37,283 --> 00:19:38,744
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what I want also.

282
00:19:39,004 --> 00:19:40,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So I can go and kill them.

283
00:19:40,384 --> 00:19:40,724
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

284
00:19:41,165 --> 00:19:41,645
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that.

285
00:19:42,758 --> 00:20:03,896
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the precept says, finish eating by noon, and noon is not specified, and you feel guilty on account of non-specificity, because your mind is infected with doubt, and instead of allowing that doubt to fully arise, even if it threatens to overwhelm you, you want to cut it up and create more precepts, and more rules, and more modes of behavior, so that this doesn't come up.

286
00:20:04,356 --> 00:20:07,719
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, so that the practice doesn't come up, because that's where the practice is.

287
00:20:11,136 --> 00:20:14,099
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why the mind would want to throw you the other way completely.

288
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:16,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, look, 50 minutes late.

289
00:20:16,061 --> 00:20:17,423
[SPEAKER_00]: You might as well go and get a wife.

290
00:20:18,744 --> 00:20:20,225
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all the same, isn't it?

291
00:20:20,406 --> 00:20:21,166
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

292
00:20:21,387 --> 00:20:25,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be throwing irrational things at you to get you to stop keeping the precepts.

293
00:20:27,573 --> 00:20:29,815
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the practice then?

294
00:20:30,316 --> 00:20:32,819
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice then is to learn the precepts and keep them.

295
00:20:33,482 --> 00:20:51,529
[SPEAKER_00]: But when anything, within the precepts, when anything, when an unwholesome hindrance comes up, do not start to aim at the more precepts and more of this, and finding why did this happen three days ago, but instead recognize that there and then, this comfort that came up is the result of rightly keeping the precepts.

296
00:20:52,729 --> 00:20:53,890
[SPEAKER_00]: And don't try to get rid of it.

297
00:20:54,290 --> 00:20:55,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to distract yourself from it.

298
00:20:55,831 --> 00:20:57,191
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the enduring on the right level.

299
00:20:57,211 --> 00:20:59,232
[SPEAKER_00]: You can live in the utmost comfort if you want.

300
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:03,502
[SPEAKER_00]: But this is the level I talk about that things should be endured at.

301
00:21:04,842 --> 00:21:07,044
[SPEAKER_00]: The tapas that the Buddha talks about, the purification.

302
00:21:07,224 --> 00:21:09,305
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like through water-washing and whatnot.

303
00:21:09,925 --> 00:21:17,910
[SPEAKER_00]: It's having these mental states there and become familiar with them, without immediately panicking and trying to resolve them.

304
00:21:20,832 --> 00:21:24,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Because doubt... what's... what's the... why is... why is doubt a problem?

305
00:21:26,936 --> 00:21:30,538
[SPEAKER_00]: There are loads of things that you don't know, loads of things you're doubtful,

306
00:21:33,790 --> 00:21:35,211
[SPEAKER_00]: But only sometimes it's a problem.

307
00:21:37,212 --> 00:21:43,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Only sometimes comes with that pressure that you want to resolve, get rid of it or whatever, manage it away.

308
00:21:43,817 --> 00:21:44,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

309
00:21:45,398 --> 00:21:49,882
[SPEAKER_00]: It's obviously not because what you don't know, because as I said, there are millions of other things you don't know.

310
00:21:52,083 --> 00:21:55,766
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because at that time, for whatever reason, it's felt painfully.

311
00:21:56,746 --> 00:22:06,893
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although it presents itself like, oh, I'm doubtful about this, if I find more information and reasons why, if I include more data about this, I will resolve it.

312
00:22:07,354 --> 00:22:09,195
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was never about the data or the lack of it.

313
00:22:09,715 --> 00:22:12,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It was about doing anything and everything to remove the pain.

314
00:22:13,538 --> 00:22:20,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And once you recognize that content of the doubt is irrelevant, it's the attitude towards the discomfort of it, that's what matters.

315
00:22:21,544 --> 00:22:24,286
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop resisting it and then you stop following the doubt.

316
00:22:24,606 --> 00:22:25,707
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you go beyond it.

317
00:22:26,490 --> 00:22:31,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Satipaṭṭhāna goes beyond doubt, not because he learned everything about everything in this world.

318
00:22:31,894 --> 00:22:37,258
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because his mind, seen through it, and cannot be moved by the presence of the discomfort of the doubt.

319
00:22:39,400 --> 00:22:41,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot make him act like he did before.

320
00:22:47,466 --> 00:22:49,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And the doubt appears in satipaṭṭhāna?

321
00:22:51,089 --> 00:22:55,452
[SPEAKER_00]: It would, in regard to things, in regard to lack of knowledge, in regard to lack of clarity.

322
00:22:55,934 --> 00:22:57,194
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't act out of it, yes.

323
00:22:58,435 --> 00:23:01,856
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't be... he won't be causing, he won't be coming out of that pressure.

324
00:23:01,956 --> 00:23:03,316
[SPEAKER_00]: See, because, as I said, he tricks you.

325
00:23:04,537 --> 00:23:13,499
[SPEAKER_00]: He wants you to act out, to get information, to resolve, to make sure this is never done again, and all of those kind of grandiose outpours, only because you don't want the pain of it.

326
00:23:15,120 --> 00:23:15,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

327
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:17,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Sotāpanna cannot resist the discomfort of it.

328
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:19,201
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what he has trained his mind to do.

329
00:23:20,301 --> 00:23:23,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus, there will be no pain that comes as a result of that resistance.

330
00:23:27,348 --> 00:23:30,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you have no problem if it comes up a hundred more times.

331
00:23:30,850 --> 00:23:34,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor will your confidence diminish, because you know it's always just going to be the same thing.

332
00:23:38,075 --> 00:23:44,819
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're aiming at removing the content of the doubt, you are doing so with the implication of, hopefully, it will never come back again.

333
00:23:45,539 --> 00:23:47,620
[SPEAKER_00]: I resolved it, finally, once and for all.

334
00:23:47,680 --> 00:23:49,842
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, five minutes later, again and again.

335
00:23:50,582 --> 00:23:51,843
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like an irrational loop.

336
00:23:52,943 --> 00:23:56,866
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, as I said, you're addressing it where it isn't the problem.

337
00:24:00,774 --> 00:24:11,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're closer to address the doubt for good, by allowing it to arise, not acting out of it, on that mental level, than to, like, learn everything that wants to direct you towards.

338
00:24:15,078 --> 00:24:23,121
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you did have people in the suttas that fully purified their mind, on account of mere faith in the basic instruction that the Buddha gave.

339
00:24:23,441 --> 00:24:28,002
[SPEAKER_03]: There was no moving parts, no 150 suttas and information.

340
00:24:28,042 --> 00:24:30,343
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just anything mentally unwholesome,

341
00:24:31,770 --> 00:24:36,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't cultivate it, don't give in to it, don't... don't try to get rid of it.

342
00:24:36,073 --> 00:24:40,376
[SPEAKER_03]: And if stuff is not unwholesome, leave it, no problem, but don't lose the sight of it.

343
00:24:41,276 --> 00:24:43,818
[SPEAKER_03]: And that was enough for them to do all the work where it matters.

344
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:50,602
[SPEAKER_05]: But you still resolve to do differently.

345
00:24:51,122 --> 00:24:51,723
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, not...

346
00:24:52,403 --> 00:25:01,229
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's not the fear of... of remorse, for the next day, going with the example of Vikalabhojana, right?

347
00:25:02,063 --> 00:25:10,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So, today... Why would you resolve to do differently if it's not really against the precepts or if it's not that clear?

348
00:25:10,788 --> 00:25:17,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't make a difference whether it's 12 or 12.15, factually, because it's not specified.

349
00:25:17,152 --> 00:25:19,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it's easier if it's 12, isn't it?

350
00:25:19,854 --> 00:25:20,154
[SPEAKER_05]: Much.

351
00:25:20,886 --> 00:25:22,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so... but it doesn't make a difference.

352
00:25:23,167 --> 00:25:26,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you feel like you're not keeping the precepts if you eat at 12.15?

353
00:25:27,030 --> 00:25:31,232
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if I feel bad today, I don't want to feel bad tomorrow.

354
00:25:31,453 --> 00:25:38,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but see, that that type of pain, if you don't want it, you don't want the right level where the practice is.

355
00:25:40,418 --> 00:25:43,939
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you try to prevent, ah, I will do this, so that this doesn't come up.

356
00:25:44,539 --> 00:25:49,500
[SPEAKER_00]: What you're saying is, I'll do random external things, so that I don't have to deal with my mind.

357
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:50,320
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll pacify it.

358
00:25:50,360 --> 00:25:52,020
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll prevent it from going there, and so on.

359
00:25:52,401 --> 00:25:53,301
[SPEAKER_00]: You already did that.

360
00:25:53,801 --> 00:25:57,622
[SPEAKER_00]: By taking on the precepts, you have created a boundary that's quite clear.

361
00:25:57,642 --> 00:26:03,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Killing, stealing, cheating, lying, if they did precepts, eating at the wrong time, and all of that.

362
00:26:03,923 --> 00:26:05,523
[SPEAKER_00]: But these are like 15 minutes.

363
00:26:05,983 --> 00:26:07,364
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... they didn't have clocks.

