01:13:37,260 --> 01:13:40,483
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, they're unbearable, not because there is no way out.

1113
01:13:41,338 --> 01:13:45,699
[SPEAKER_00]: They're unbearable because you crave against not having the way out.

1114
01:13:46,419 --> 01:13:50,261
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop craving, then the confinement, as the Suttas say, becomes the opening.

1115
01:13:51,041 --> 01:13:53,742
[SPEAKER_00]: That which was confining you is actually what the freedom is.

1116
01:13:56,002 --> 01:13:58,383
[SPEAKER_00]: The impermanence is actually what frees you from burden.

1117
01:13:59,043 --> 01:14:01,644
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you can't own things even if you want to.

1118
01:14:03,784 --> 01:14:05,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But for that, you need to confine yourself first.

1119
01:14:20,788 --> 01:14:23,749
[SPEAKER_03]: And there's actually a sutta that...

1120
01:14:25,030 --> 01:14:26,751
[SPEAKER_03]: I think it was like a monk.

1121
01:14:28,092 --> 01:14:28,772
[SPEAKER_03]: It wasn't the Buddha.

1122
01:14:29,932 --> 01:14:31,913
[SPEAKER_03]: And he was talking to a king's charioteer.

1123
01:14:33,254 --> 01:14:44,039
[SPEAKER_03]: And the king's charioteer said, oh, I heard that your teacher, he teaches about, you know, freedom like from confinement, openings amidst the confinement.

1124
01:14:45,600 --> 01:14:49,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he asked him like, yes, but what do you understand by confinement?

1125
